# Prove the result of a RUNTIME-CHOSEN operation over a private value — which function runs is
# selected by a private flag, not fixed in the source.
#
#   glass prove examples/prove/computed_callee.glass mode=1 x=21   # -> 42 (double), ACCEPT
#   glass prove examples/prove/computed_callee.glass mode=0 x=21   # -> 22 (increment), ACCEPT
#
# The bridge could already inline a NAMED function passed as an argument (v5.87: map(inc, xs)), but
# it refused a callee CHOSEN AT RUNTIME — `(if mode >= 1 then double else increment)(x)` — the last
# refused call form. The cut: push the application INSIDE the selector. Applying a conditionally-
# chosen function equals conditionally applying each candidate, so `(if c then g else h)(x)` lowers
# as `if c then g(x) else h(x)` — two ordinary named-fn calls, muxed by the same gadget an `if`
# already uses. Sound because Glass is pure (no effect is duplicated or reordered). The rewrite is
# shared by the guard (seval) and the circuit (unroll → heval/cgen), so they can't disagree.
# mode and x stay private; only the result is revealed, and the Third Witness confirms it.
fn double(x: Int) : Int = x + x
fn increment(x: Int) : Int = x + 1

fn run(mode: Int, x: Int) : Int = (if mode >= 1 then double else increment)(x)

run(mode, x)
